      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(plugins): honor empty contract plugin scopes",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nBundled manifest contract resolution treated `onlyPluginIds: []` the same as an omitted scope. That expanded an explicit empty plugin scope into all eligible bundled contract plugins.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe plugin scope helper already preserves the difference between \"unscoped\" and \"explicitly empty.\" This change uses that shared helper in bundled manifest contract resolution instead of a local helper that collapsed empty scopes.\n\n## User Impact\n\nCallers that intentionally pass an empty plugin scope now get no bundled contract owners, instead of accidentally loading enabled bundled plugins.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Changed `src/plugins/bundled-manifest-contract-plugins.ts`.\n- Added `src/plugins/bundled-manifest-contract-plugins.test.ts` proving unscoped resolution still returns the bundled contract owner while `onlyPluginIds: []` returns none.\n- `OPENCLAW_HEAVY_CHECK_LOCK_SCOPE=worktree node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/plugins/bundled-manifest-contract-plugins.test.ts`\n- `git diff --check upstream/main..HEAD`\n- Controlled resolver proof: `node --import tsx - <<'EOF' ...` invoked the real bundled manifest contract resolver for `documentExtractors` with omitted scope and with `onlyPluginIds: []`.\n\n## Proof\n\n- Controlled proof with omitted scope returned the real bundled document extractor owner: `unscopedCount: 1`, sample `document-extract`.\n- Controlled proof with `onlyPluginIds: []` returned no owners: `emptyScopeCount: 0`, `emptyScope: []`.\n- This proves explicit empty plugin scope no longer falls through to unscoped bundled discovery.\n",
        "branch": "llagy007/plugin-only-plugin-ids-empty",
        "head_sha": "df2ada728c4173f435f5841d57574adcd6281e16",
        "author": "llagy007",
